\fs96 \cf2 Ownership\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A challenge you might face when using some programming languages is that you must manually allocate and deallocate memory. When multiple parts of the program need access to the same memory, it becomes difficult to keep track of who "owns" a value and determine when is the right time to deallocate it. If you make a mistake, it may result in a "use-after-free" error, a "double free" error, or a "leaked memory" error, any one of which can be catastrophic.\
Mojo helps avoid these errors by ensuring there is only one variable that owns each value at a time, while still allowing you to share references with other functions. When the life span of the owner ends, Mojo {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 destroys the value}}. Programmers are still responsible for making sure any type that allocates resources (including memory) also deallocates those resources in its destructor. Mojo's ownership system ensures that destructors are called promptly.\
On this page, we'll explain the rules that govern this ownership model, and how to specify different argument conventions that define how values are passed into functions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Ownership summary\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The fundamental rules that make Mojo's ownership model work are the following:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Every value has only one owner at a time.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When the lifetime of the owner ends, Mojo destroys the value.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If there are existing references to a value, Mojo extends the lifetime of the owner.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Variables and references\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A variable owns its value. A struct owns its fields.\
A reference allows you to access a value owned by another variable. A reference has either mutable access or immutable access to that value.\
Mojo references are created when you call a function: function arguments are passed as mutable or immutable references. A function can return a reference instead of returning a value. To capture a returned reference, you can use a reference binding:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 ref\cf13  value_ref \cf5 =\cf13  \cf14 list\cf5 [\cf15 0\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Argument conventions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In all programming languages, code quality and performance is heavily dependent upon how functions treat argument values. That is, whether a value received by a function is a unique value or a reference, and whether it's mutable or immutable, has a series of consequences that define the readability, performance, and safety of the language.\
In Mojo, we want to provide full {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/value-semantics/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 value semantics}} by default, which provides consistent and predictable behavior. But as a systems programming language, we also need to offer full control over memory optimizations, which generally requires reference semantics. The trick is to introduce reference semantics in a way that ensures all code is memory safe by tracking the lifetime of every value and destroying each one at the right time (and only once). All of this is made possible in Mojo through the use of argument conventions that ensure every value has only one owner at a time.\
An argument convention specifies whether an argument is mutable or immutable, and whether the function owns the value. Each convention is defined by a keyword at the beginning of an argument declaration:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
read
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : The function receives an immutable reference. This means the function can read the original value (it's not a copy), but it can't mutate (modify) it.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : The function receives a mutable reference. This means the function can read and mutate the original value (it's not a copy).\
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : The function takes ownership of a value. This means the function has exclusive ownership of the argument. The caller might choose to transfer ownership of an existing value to this function, but that's not always what happens. The callee might receive a newly-created value, or a copy of an existing value.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : The function gets a reference with a parametric mutability: that is, the reference is either mutable or immutable. You can think of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  arguments as a generalization of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 read
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conventions. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  arguments are an advanced topic, and they're described in more detail in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/lifetimes/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Lifetimes, origins, and references}}.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : A special convention used for the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#constructor"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 constructors}} and for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/#named-results"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 named results}}. An 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument is uninitialized at the beginning of the function, and must be initialized before the function returns. Although 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  arguments show up in the argument list, they're never passed in by the caller.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : A special convention used in the destructor and consuming-move lifecycle methods. A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument is initialized at the beginning of the function, and uninitialized when the function returns.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For example, this function has one argument that's a mutable reference and one that's immutable:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 add\cf5 (mut\cf13  x\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 read\cf13  y\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     x \cf5 +=\cf13  y\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  a \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 1\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  b \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 2\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     add\cf5 (\cf13 a\cf5 ,\cf13  b\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf13 a\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 3\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You've probably already seen some function arguments that don't declare a convention. By default, all arguments are 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 read
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . In the following sections, we'll explain each of these argument conventions in more detail.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Immutable arguments (
\fs56 \cf10 \cb18 read
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1 )\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 read
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention is the default for all arguments. The callee receives an immutable reference to the argument value.\
For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 print_list\cf5 (\cf14 list\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ])\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf14 list\cf5 .\cf13 __str__\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  values \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 4\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     print_list\cf5 (\cf13 values\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 [1, 2, 3, 4]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Here the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 print_list()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function can read from the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 list
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument, but not mutate it. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 list
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a reference to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 values
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function, not a copy.\
In general, passing an immutable reference is much more efficient when handling large or expensive-to-copy values, because the copy constructor and destructor aren't invoked for a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 read
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Compared to C++ and Rust\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo's read argument convention is similar in some ways to passing an argument by 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 const&
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in C++, which also avoids a copy of the value and disables mutability in the callee. However, the read convention differs from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 const&
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in C++ in two important ways:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Mojo compiler implements a lifetime checker that ensures that values are not destroyed when there are outstanding references to those values.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Small values like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Float
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are always passed in machine registers. This provides a significant performance enhancement compared to languages like C++ and Rust.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The major difference between Rust and Mojo is that Mojo doesn't require a sigil on the caller side to pass by immutable reference. Also, Mojo is more efficient when passing small values, and Rust defaults to moving values instead of passing them around by borrow. These policy and syntax decisions allow Mojo to provide an easier-to-use programming model.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Mutable arguments (
\fs56 \cf10 \cb18 mut
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1 )\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you'd like your function to receive a mutable reference, add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword in front of the argument name. You can think of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  like this: it means any changes to the value inside the function are visible outside the function.\
For example, this 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mutate()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function updates the original 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 list
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 print_list\cf5 (\cf14 list\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ])\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf14 list\cf5 .\cf13 __str__\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 mutate\cf5 (mut\cf13  l\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ])\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     l\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf15 5\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  values \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 4\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12     mutate\cf5 (\cf13 values\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     print_list\cf5 (\cf13 values\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 That behaves like an optimized replacement for this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 print_list\cf5 (\cf14 list\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ])\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf14 list\cf5 .\cf13 __str__\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 mutate_copy\cf5 (\cf13 l\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ])\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ]:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf6 # def creates an implicit copy of the list because it's mutated\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     l\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf15 5\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 return\cf13  l\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  values \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 4\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12     values \cf5 =\cf13  mutate_copy\cf5 (\cf13 values\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     print_list\cf5 (\cf13 values\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

