\fs32 \cf2 Although the code using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  isn't that much shorter, it's more memory efficient because it doesn't make a copy of the value.\
However, remember that the values passed as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  must already be mutable. For example, if you try to take a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 read
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value and pass it to another function as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you'll get a compiler error because Mojo can't form a mutable reference from an immutable reference.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 You can't define {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/#optional-arguments"}}{\fldrslt \ul default values}} for 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 mut
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  arguments.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Argument exclusivity\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo enforces argument exclusivity for mutable references. This means that if a function receives a mutable reference to a value (such as an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument), it can't receive any other references to the same value\'97mutable or immutable. That is, a mutable reference can't have any other references that alias it.\
For example, consider the following code example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 append_twice\cf5 (mut\cf13  s\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 ,\cf13  other\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12    \cf6 # Mojo knows 's' and 'other' can't be the same string.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12    s \cf5 +=\cf13  other\cb1 \
\cb12    s \cf5 +=\cf13  other\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 invalid_access\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12   \cf5 var\cf13  my_string \cf5 =\cf13  \cf21 "o"\cf13   \cf6 # Create a run-time String value\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12   \cf6 # error: passing `my_string` mut is invalid since it's also passed\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12   \cf6 # read.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12   append_twice\cf5 (\cf13 my_string\cf5 ,\cf13  my_string\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12   \cf5 print(\cf13 my_string\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This code is confusing because the user might expect the output to be 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ooo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but since the first addition mutates both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 s
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 other
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the actual output would be 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 oooo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Enforcing exclusivity of mutable references not only prevents coding errors, it also allows the Mojo compiler to optimize code in some cases.\
One way to avoid this issue when you do need both a mutable and an immutable reference (or need to pass the same value to two arguments) is to make a copy:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 valid_access\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12   \cf5 var\cf13  my_string \cf5 =\cf13  \cf21 "o"\cf13            \cf6 # Create a run-time String value\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12   \cf5 var\cf13  other_string \cf5 =\cf13  my_string  \cf6 # Create a copy of the String value\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12   append_twice\cf5 (\cf13 my_string\cf5 ,\cf13  other_string\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12   \cf5 print(\cf13 my_string\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Note that argument exclusivity isn't enforced for register-passable trivial types (like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Bool
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) as they're always passed by copy. When passing the same value into two 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  arguments, the callee receives two copies of the value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Transfer arguments (
\fs56 \cf10 \cb18 var
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb18 ^
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1 )\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 And finally, if you'd like your function to receive value ownership, add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword in front of the argument name.\
This convention is often combined with use of the postfixed 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  "transfer" sigil on the variable that is passed into the function, which ends the lifetime of that variable.\
Technically, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword doesn't guarantee that the received value is the original value\'97it guarantees only that the function gets unique ownership of a value. This happens in one of three ways:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The caller passes the argument with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  transfer sigil, which ends the lifetime of that variable (the variable becomes uninitialized) and ownership is transferred into the function.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The caller doesn't use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  transfer sigil, in which case, Mojo copies the value. If the type isn't copyable, this is a compile-time error.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The caller passes in a newly-created "owned" value, such as a value returned from a function. In this case, no variable owns the value and it's transferred directly to the callee. For example:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 take\cf5 (var\cf13  s\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 )\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf13 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf5 pass\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf13 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    take\cf5 (\cf21 "A brand-new String!"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf13 \cb12 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf13 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The following code works by making a copy of the string, because 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 take_text()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  uses the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention, and the caller doesn't include the transfer sigil:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 take_text\cf5 (var\cf13  text\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     text \cf5 +=\cf13  \cf21 "!"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf13 text\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  message \cf5 =\cf13  \cf21 "Hello"\cf13   \cf6 # Create a run-time String value\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     take_text\cf5 (\cf13 message\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf13 message\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 Hello!\cb1 \
\cb12 Hello\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 However, if you add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  transfer sigil when calling 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 take_text()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the compiler complains about 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 print(message)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , because at that point, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 message
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable is no longer initialized. That is, this version doesn't compile:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  message \cf5 =\cf13  \cf21 "Hello"\cf13   \cf6 # Create a run-time String value\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     take_text\cf5 (\cf13 message\cf5 ^)\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf13 message\cf5 )\cf13   \cf6 # error: use of uninitialized value 'message'\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This is a critical feature of Mojo's lifetime checker, because it ensures that no two variables have ownership of the same value. To fix the error, you must not use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 message
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable after you end its lifetime with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  transfer sigil. So here is the corrected code:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 take_text\cf5 (var\cf13  text\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     text \cf5 +=\cf13  \cf21 "!"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf13 text\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  message \cf5 =\cf13  \cf21 "Hello"\cf13   \cf6 # Create a run-time String value\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     take_text\cf5 (\cf13 message\cf5 ^)\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 Hello!\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Regardless of how it receives the value, when the function declares an argument as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , it's certain that it has unique mutable access to that value. Because the value is owned, the value is destroyed when the function exits\'97unless the function transfers the value elsewhere.\
For example, in the following example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 add_to_list()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  takes a string and appends it to the list. Ownership of the string is transferred to the list, so it's not destroyed when the function exits. On the other hand, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 consume_string()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  doesn't transfer its 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value out, so the value is destroyed at the end of the function.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 add_to_list\cf5 (var\cf13  name\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 mut\cf13  \cf14 list\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 String\cf5 ])\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf14 list\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf13 name\cf5 ^)\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf6 # name is uninitialized, nothing to destroy\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 consume_string\cf5 (var\cf13  s\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 )\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf13 s\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf6 # s is destroyed here\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Transfer implementation details\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In Mojo, you shouldn't conflate "ownership transfer" with a "move operation"\'97these aren't strictly the same thing.\
There are multiple ways that Mojo transfers ownership of a value:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If a type implements the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#move-constructor"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 move constructor}}, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __init__(take=)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , Mojo may invoke this method if a value of that type is transferred into a function as a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument, and the original variable's lifetime ends at the same point (with or without use of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  transfer sigil).\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
In some cases, Mojo optimizes away the move operation entirely, leaving the value in the same memory location but updating its ownership. In these cases, a value transfers without invoking either the copy or move constructors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In order for the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention to work without the transfer sigil, the value type must be copyable (via 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __init__(out self, *, copy: Self)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri960\partightenfactor0
